A||||19||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||98 Saturn DOHC swap||||1||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||06-28-2001||01:13 AM||||I was considering a 3800 series I swap for my 84 Fiero, but I saw some pictures of the DOHC saturn.<P>It looks like it would be possible to put the Saturn DOHC and transmission in a Fiero.  Has anybody done this?<P>I am looking for reliability and easy of maintainence.  The V6 eniges are a tight squeeze.  On the other hand, the Iron Duke is a bit fragile.  I want a modern 4 cylinder that is light, efficient, and more powerful than an Iron Duke.<P>So, please don't remind me that the 3800 is a faster/better choice.  I have one already in a crate in my driveway.  However, squeezing it into my Fiero is possible but I worry about the modifications (cutting decklid hinge, no room for torque movement, etc)<P>So, I am considering a 2.4L DOHC from a sunfire or cavalier too.<P>How would a Saturn DOHC comprae to the 2.4L DOHC?<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||venger||06-28-2001||02:05 AM||||The 3.8 is has more room than the 2.8, alot fast, and alot more reliable that the 2.8.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||06-28-2001||05:21 AM||||I have been, ah, unimpressed with saturn engines power output.<P>The 3800 will goooo, and many others have done the swap, and their cars are great  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000003||JJ-85-2M6||06-28-2001||04:41 PM||||With a manual transmission the Saturn DOHC might seem like enough power for everyday driving. With an automatic, I don't think it would be worth the swap. My DOHC (that I have had since new) has a bit of power, but I have the Automatic with a performance/economy shift mode.  Another member of my family has a DOHC with AT without that switch. It is newer, but has considerably less pickup than mine.  Also, with AC turned on the cars accelerate very slowly. Just my 2 cents!<P><BR>J<p>[This message has been edited by JJ-85-2M6 (edited 06-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||pherder||06-28-2001||05:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LuckyTheyWereEverMade:<BR><B>So, I am considering a 2.4L DOHC from a sunfire or cavalier too. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The TwinCam is a newer version of the LO Quad 4.  Do a search on "Quad" for several threads.<P><B>ANY</B> engine swap that was <B>NOT</B> stock to the Fiero will require a lot of work.<P>You would think that a Quad 4 engine being a inline 4 cylinder would have plenty of room to be worked on.  <P><B>NOT!!!</B><P>I had to remove the injectors ... got them off but to get them back on I had to drop the engine out of the car!!!<P>Paul<P>||||reg||13
Z||000005||TRiAD||06-28-2001||07:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LuckyTheyWereEverMade:<BR><B>...I am considering a 2.4L DOHC from a sunfire or cavalier too...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ther are SEVERAL companies that have BIG performance parts for the late Q4s. They have 150hp from the factory, and an SC'ed version with over 250hp is not at all unrealistic. <P>I would personally avoid the Saturn. <P>Good luck!<P><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000006||filthyscarecrow||06-29-2001||02:28 AM||||hey, we have a couple of those 2.4's lying around up in the mech. eng. dept here at the U. i doubt they'll ever use them. they also have a cosworth rally 4cyl supercharged that i am wondering if they'll miss. hmm. and quite a few turbos too. man, i gotta start swiping some of this stuff. they just have it donated so they wno't care. one of those 2.4s has been sitting in the hallway on 4th floor for the last 3 months... hmm, i wonder if that cosworth will fit in my backpack : )<P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||06-29-2001||09:54 AM||||the DOHC 4 cylinder engine from Saturn is only 125HP or so. Less than the 2.8L V6 that is stock in a Fiero.<P>  granted you can get an almost new saturn engine - and it should be more reliable than an old or rebuilt fiero motor - I think the saturn engine will be lighter<P> so even though you are swapping in equal HP the lighter engine would give you some increase in performance.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||06-29-2001||09:57 AM||||The Saturn TwinCam is NOT the same as the 2.4 TwinCam.<P>Saturn uses two DOHC I4 engines: the 1.9 TwinCam and the GM's 2.2 litre global 4 cylinder.<P>Saturn does NOT use the 2.4 litre TwinCam used in Sunfires, Cavaliers, etc.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Standard||06-29-2001||12:47 PM||||Hey filthy, grab one of those turbo's for me!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  One of the 2.4's would be nice, too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/wacko.gif"> <BR>1986 GT 5 speed<BR>1984 SC Auto<P>AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||06-29-2001||01:19 PM||||Ivelost track of what saturn is putting in their cars now<P>it use to be the single overhead cam 100 hp motor and the DOHC 125 HP motor.<P> but now they have the L series out and I think the 4 cylinder in that is different from the one in their other cars - and they also have a V6 for the L series.||||reg||1
Z||000011||V8Dreamer||06-29-2001||02:59 PM||||I wouldn't waste my time with a four cylinder ever again.....plus, what's the torque on one of those little saturn 4 bangers?  I'm assuming it's probably close to the same as my John Deere......<BR>But then again, this is just my opinion, maybe it'd be worth it to you for econo reasons etc.<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000012||filthyscarecrow||06-29-2001||03:24 PM||||i think i really should start asking where the engines and turbos and superchargers go when they're done with them. my guess is that they're sell it for cheap. i think a lot of it ends up on ebay. i know that's what we did with the R6 yamaha parts from the bike we got the engines out of for the formula car... ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||06-29-2001||03:45 PM||||econo reasons - hey now you are onto something. my 2000 saturn SL with the 100HP motor and 5 speed gets 45mph highway.<P> when the fiero was first proposed to GM the engineers said they were targeting 50mpg.<P>  the original fiero 4 cylinder was 90, 95hp - so putting the 100hp saturn engine in might just acheive 50mpg with the improved aerodynamics over the saturn SL, and the larger tires.<P> Building a 100HP 4 cylinder 50mpg fiero WOULD be a milestone - the original concept finally realized (after 17 years).<P>  and it would be fun to take your fiero to a saturn dealer and ask them to "take a look at my engine" then pop the lid and watch their eyes do the same.<P>  At least you'd get a free doughnut out of it!<P>edit: and the saturn engine is so quiet already - with it mounted on the isolated subframe the drivetrain would be almost SILENT at 70mph - now that would be unique in a fiero too!<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Posthumane||06-29-2001||05:16 PM||||Silent? Fiero? Those two don't seem to fit together in the same sentence. It would be an interesting swap tho, atleast you'd have an engine with some tech behind it, compared to the duke. btw filthy, what year do you do the formula car? i'm going to u of calgary in the fall, and i want to get into that.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Coupe'N||06-29-2001||07:09 PM||||HEY FILTHYSCARECROW.... Find out what they DO do with them and let us know. I'm sure you could throw a couple this way and that!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Coupe'N outt!!!||||reg||6
Z||000016||Tryxalon||06-29-2001||11:31 PM||||On the SL Sreries (a compact 2100 lb car) <P>Saturn SOHC Engine  1,901-cc, SOHC, 8-valve, inline-4, alloy block and head<BR> <BR>Horsepower 100 @ 5,000 rpm  Torque (ft. lbs.) 114 @ 2,400 rpm<BR> <BR>Saturn DOHC Engine 1,901-cc, DOHC, 16-valve, inline-4, alloy block and head<BR> <BR>Horsepower 124 @ 5,600 rpm   Torque (ft. lbs.) 122 @ 4,800 rpm<P>These would fit in the compartment, but honestly, the 151 Duke with 92 HP and 145 Torque is a better engine for the car since it has a broad torque curve, not "spikey" like the Saturn engines (similar to Hondas!!) <P><BR>In the slightly Larger LS series (from the European Opel Vectra)<P>Saturn 2,198-cc, DOHC, 16-valve, inline-4, alloy cylinder head and block<BR> <BR>Horsepower 135 @ 5,200 rpm Torque (ft. lbs.) 142 @ 4,400 rpm<BR> <BR>would be a better choice .. BUT the mounts are totally different. Engine Front, the single transmission mount ... and a front of engine anti-torque Mount (above the serpentine!) This mount would have to mount between the battery and the shock tower (yep, you'd have to move the battery And the wiring harness to make a mount for it.) <P>The V-6 in the LS series is: <BR> 3,000-cc, DOHC, 24-valve, V-6, alloy cylinder head<BR> <BR>Horsepower 182 @ 5,600 rpm Torque (ft. lbs.) 190 @ 3,600 rpm<BR> <BR>But I have no idea of it's mountings!! <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 06-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ken Wittlief||06-30-2001||12:24 AM||||since the new V6 goes in the L series the mounts for it must be similar to the mounts for the 4 cylinder in the L series, i would expect them to be anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000018||filthyscarecrow||06-30-2001||07:02 PM||||the formula car project is just that- an extracurricular project. it's not for any specific year. the guy that designed our dry-sump oiling system is like 30 years old and still a student. it's a great project to be on too, i've learned more in the last 9 months than my whole life about cars, and i knew quite a bit going into it. you can look at our website at <A HREF="http://www.tc.umn.edu/~sae" TARGET=_blank>www.tc.umn.edu/~sae</A>  or at <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/umnfsae2002" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/umnfsae2002</A>  (this is our site for the upcoming car.) hey, maybe i'll see you in detroit next year...<P>as for the turbos and such, i asked and i guess they just stockpile that stuff. our storage area is next to CDR where they work on the turbos, and i guess there are an arseload of them in our storage bay. i'll have to ask someone who has the authority to sell them...<BR>i still want the rally cosworth 4cyl. they said that was rated at like 500hp when they tested it... ||||reg||1
